[h1]Speculative Markets[/h1]
Speculative Markets adds financial booms, bank lending and market crashes to Victoria 3. Investors chase rising prices, banks lend into the boom, and it can get ugly when prices turn. Your laws, credit policy and decisions decide how far it goes and who pays.

Every country also gets an Exchange, with its own charter, specialities, competitors and place in the world rankings.

Tested on [b]Victoria 3 1.15.1[/b]. Supports running campaigns and earlier mod saves.

Try updates early on [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3839275304]Beta[/url]. [b]Enable one channel at a time.[/b]

[h1]Interactive Tutorial[/h1]
[url=https://claude.ai/artifact/RK4Lrf6U1rU7HC8Vx5pDvy][b]Open the interactive tutorial in your browser[/b][/url]
See booms, bank lending, leverage and panics play out month by month, pull the same levers you have in the game, and take the guided tour. It runs the mod's own formulas, so it's a quick way to learn how it all fits together.

[h2]Getting started[/h2]
Open [b]Finance[/b] on the left sidebar. Hover over a value to see what affects it.

[h2]Trading Floor[/h2]
[img]https://i.imgur.com/9RsQgg4.png[/img]
Your Exchange grows from Provincial to National and International standing. The world's top three are World Centres, with more influence and smaller losses from foreign panics. Higher standing unlocks more Specialities.

Pick a charter: a [b]Commodity Exchange[/b] for stronger booms, a [b]Money Market[/b] for steadier trust, or a [b]Share Market[/b] for more credit. [b]Paths to Renown[/b] let you race other Exchanges in your Specialities, such as Futures Pit, Lender to Nations and Railway Mania. Higher places pay more, but every path has a drawback.

[h2]Blue Chips[/h2]
A company that stays prosperous for a year becomes a [b]blue chip[/b]. Blue chips soften the confidence lost to collapsing booms and foreign panics, add trust, raise Capitalist investment and pull your contests your way. Losing one costs trust and confidence. Each year the most prosperous is named [b]Company of the Year[/b].

[h2]Speculation[/h2]
[img]https://i.imgur.com/IhomjxQ.png[/img]
[b]Market Confidence[/b] runs from 0 to 100. Booms raise it; crashes, debt and lost wars bring it down. Above 80, investors become reckless. Below your Panic Threshold, a Financial Panic starts.

Staying above 75 builds [b]Speculative Excess[/b], shown as a bar. It cools between 60 and 75, faster under Tight credit. If confidence falls below 60, the bubble pops and costs extra confidence, or Bad Debts once confidence runs out.

During a panic, output and tax revenue fall, banks call in loans, and private construction is cut by half, or more at high leverage. The treasury also has to cover relief for the unemployed.

Each good has its own [b]Investor Interest[/b], from Fleeing to Feverish. Hot goods get more investment but crash harder. Restrain a hot good, encourage a cold one, or float a Great Issue to raise your Exchange score at more risk.
[h2]Banking[/h2]
[img]https://i.imgur.com/FHX0Gaq.png[/img]
Credit helps your economy grow, but too much makes a panic harder to recover from. Each month of [b]Credit Leverage[/b] adds 2% throughput, up to 24%. Beyond 3 months, the Panic Threshold rises, and panics last longer, cut deeper into construction and leave Bad Debts that reduce throughput and investment for years.

The [b]Lending Limit[/b] starts at 3 months of investment pool income and can rise to 12. Credit policy (Tight, Normal or Loose) sets how freely banks lend. [b]Lending Standards[/b], from Strict to Reckless, drift looser in good times and tighten after crashes.

In a panic, banks pull back half your outstanding credit, and the higher your leverage, the more of the investment pool they can take.

[b]Lend to the Banks[/b] shortens a panic and softens its penalties. You can buy up bad loans afterwards, or pledge a cheaper automatic rescue beforehand.

[h2]Exchanges[/h2]
[img]https://i.imgur.com/L2xgPYG.png[/img]
Compare every Exchange at National standing or above. The top five receive prestige and a higher Lending Limit.

Each Exchange has [b]contest slots[/b]: one, plus one per Standing above Provincial, up to four. Start a contest from the Leading Exchanges list or by right-clicking a country on the map. You need at least Engaged interest in the strategic region of their capital, and you cannot contest an Exchange that is already contesting you.

Contests settle after five years. A lead under 20% is a draw. At 75% or more, a decisive win attracts available capital from the loser's investment pool for a year. Quitting early counts as a loss at the current standings.

Push with a [b]Bear Raid[/b], [b]Lure Their Capital[/b] or a [b]Press Campaign[/b]. [b]Hold the Line[/b] undoes a push. Each of these moves has a one-year cooldown per competitor. [b]Shore Up[/b] halves a competitor's moves and can be renewed after two years. AI Exchanges play by the same rules.

[h2]Interest Groups[/h2]
Each interest group favors about two goods your country trades and may oppose one. Booms, busts and your interventions in those goods move its approval. See them in the interest group panel and on the Speculation tab.

[h2]Monetary Standard[/h2]
[b]Bimetallism[/b] makes bank rescues cheaper but booms end harder. The [b]Gold Standard[/b] cuts loan interest and trust losses but makes panics longer. [b]Fiat Money[/b] shortens panics but feeds the bubble and costs trust. Laissez-Faire and Interventionism each turn one into a stronger variant.

[h2]Financial Integration[/h2]
With Sphere of Influence, power blocs can adopt Financial Integration. Its three levels shelter members from each other's panics, share the leader's Lending Limit and feed the leader's Exchange.

[h2]Other features[/h2]
[list]
[*]A Great Power's panic can spread through trade and foreign ownership. From around 1870, it can develop into a worldwide Long Depression.
[*]The Financial Markets journal entry opens a map showing market conditions in each country. Stripes mark countries a panic has recently spread to.
[*]Economic system and trade policy laws affect the markets. Their tooltips list the effects. Speculation is disabled under Command Economy or Cooperative Ownership.
[*]Alerts below the top bar warn you about approaching panics and speculative bubbles.
[*]The Market Volatility game rule has a High setting if you want more volatile markets.
[/list]

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]
[list]
[*]Using CMF? Add the [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3839275348]CMF patch[/url] and load it last.
[*]Using Morgenroete? Add the [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3839705018]Morgenroete patch[/url]. Disable the old Speculative Markets+Morgenroete patch.
[*]Using Tech & Res? Add the [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3841713072]Tech & Res patch[/url]: [b]CMF > Tech & Res > Speculative Markets > Tech & Res patch > CMF patch.[/b] It covers Morgenroete's goods too, so use it instead of the Morgenroete patch.
[/list]
With CMF and Morgenroete, load [b]CMF > Morgenroete > Speculative Markets > Morgenroete patch > CMF patch.[/b]

Mods that replace the sidebar, goods panel, interest group panel or building definitions may need a patch.

For multiplayer, everyone needs the mod.

Available in English, Brazilian Portuguese, French, German, Japanese, Korean, Polish, Russian, Simplified Chinese, Spanish and Turkish. Translations from English are machine translated. Please let me know if something reads badly.

[h2]Feedback[/h2]
Feedback from longer campaigns helps tune panic frequency and severity.

Report bugs in the comments or bug discussion. Include versions, other mods, and what happened. Screenshots and error logs help.

